a:2:{s:7:"current";a:7:{s:4:"date";a:2:{s:7:"created";i:1424796798;s:8:"modified";i:1424796798;}s:7:"creator";s:9:"Rob Lloyd";s:4:"user";s:8:"shelgame";s:11:"last_change";a:7:{s:4:"date";i:1424796798;s:2:"ip";s:15:"205.144.100.200";s:4:"type";s:1:"C";s:2:"id";s:21:"switchableboost_setup";s:4:"user";s:8:"shelgame";s:3:"sum";s:7:"created";s:5:"extra";s:0:"";}s:8:"internal";a:2:{s:5:"cache";b:1;s:3:"toc";b:1;}s:11:"description";a:1:{s:8:"abstract";s:421:"Switchable boost

This allows a ‘LOW’ boost setting to be used when a switch is applied. It must be one of the existing inputs to the SMEC (Cruise, A/C, P/N switch, etc.). But, these can be ‘doubled up’ – IE, you can have the cruise on/off switch work for both hi/lo boost as well as cruise. This is similar in operation to the S60 boost switch feature, though without the timing adjustment for low octane fuel.";}s:8:"relation";a:1:{s:10:"firstimage";s:0:"";}}s:10:"persistent";a:4:{s:4:"date";a:1:{s:7:"created";i:1424796798;}s:7:"creator";s:9:"Rob Lloyd";s:4:"user";s:8:"shelgame";s:11:"last_change";a:7:{s:4:"date";i:1424796798;s:2:"ip";s:15:"205.144.100.200";s:4:"type";s:1:"C";s:2:"id";s:21:"switchableboost_setup";s:4:"user";s:8:"shelgame";s:3:"sum";s:7:"created";s:5:"extra";s:0:"";}}}